gm-method/rendering: an adaptive-quality controller (dynamic pixel-ratio / resolution / AA that drops on slow frames and raises on fast ones) OSCILLATES ~1-2Hz when the drop and raise thresholds are close AND the device sits right between them at the two quality levels. Mechanism: at the HIGH setting the frame is just over the drop budget (e.g. avg ~17ms > 17ms drop), so it drops; at the LOW setting it is just under the raise budget (e.g. ~15ms < 15ms raise), so after a fixed dwell it raises again -> back into the drop zone -> repeat. The fixed re-engage dwell guarantees a steady period (~dwell). Each quality switch that calls renderer.setPixelRatio/setSize is a RENDERER RESIZE that re-allocates render targets -- so any FBO-backed effect (a half-res water/atmosphere composite, bloom, SSAO) goes BLANK for a frame on every toggle, which the user sees as 'X flickers on and off twice a second'. FIX: EXPONENTIAL DWELL BACKOFF -- count consecutive quick re-drops (dropped again within ~2*dwell of re-engaging) and double the re-engage dwell (cap it) so a device that genuinely cannot hold the high setting SETTLES at the low one instead of toggling; reset the backoff after a sustained-stable stretch at the high setting so a transient slow patch doesn't pin quality down forever. A static-threshold hysteresis gap alone does NOT fix it when the device's two-level frame times straddle the gap -- the controller must learn to give up re-engaging. Witness live by sampling the controller's published debug state (engaged/pixelRatio) over a few seconds: count state transitions before (~2-3/4s) and after (0) and confirm it settled at one ratio.